2025-07-11 14:20:17 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:165 - 创建分块 11: 数据 = {'id': 'de0a3906-890f-464f-b5c4-3885652bbc6c', 'document_id': '86b265a7-bbbb-4661-8c50-f4da9fbcd61e', 'content': 'be x-ray amorphous with a pronounced hump at ${28}^{ \\circ }{2\\Theta }$ , which confirmed geopolymer formation devoid of any crystalline phases at room temperature. ![01973e1a-dfe2-7052-97bf-a3fb626b5a97_7_271_1044_756_450_0.jpg](images/01973e1a-dfe2-7052-97bf-a3fb626b5a97_7_271_1044_756_450_0.jpg) Figure 7. XRD pattern of bamboo-reinforced geopolymer composite. ## CONCLUSIONS This preliminary investigation reinforcing geopolymer composites with untreated bamboo fibers resulted in the following: 1) SEM micrographs revealed a weak interface between the fibers and the geopolymer matrix. However, the stress-strain curves were very smooth, and yielded a reasonable flexural strength of ${4.63}\\mathrm{{MPa}}$ .\n2) X-ray diffraction pattern confirmed amorphous geopolymer formation with a hump at ${28}^{ \\circ }$ 2Θ.\n3) The bamboo-reinforced geopolymer composite is a potential sustainable green material for construction.\n## ACKNOWLEDGEMENTS\nBamboo collection and fibers preparation and conditioning were done at the Structural Engineering Laboratory (LTEE-INPA) and at the Particleboard Laboratory (LA-INPA). SEM and XRD were carried out at the Frederick Seitz Materials Research Laboratory Central Facilities (FS-MRL). Flexural strength tests were carried out at the Advanced Mechanical Testing and Evaluation Laboratory of UIUC by Kaushik Sankar. This work was partially supported by the US Air Force Office of Scientific Research (AFOSR) through the Tyndall Air Force base in Florida under Grant No. FA 8650-11-1-5900. REFERENCES\n${}^{1}$ Davidovits, J. (1991). Geopolymers - inorganic polymeric new materials. J. Therm. Anal. 37(8):1633-1656.\n${}^{2}$ Davidovits, J. (2002). 30 years of success and failures in geopolymer applications - market trends and potential breakthroughs. In: Geopolymer 2002 Conference Keynotes, October 28-29, Melbourne, Australia, pp 1-15.\n${}^{3}$ Kriven, W.M. (2010). Inorganic polysialates or "geopolymers". American Ceramic Society Bulletin 89(4):31-34.', 'chunk_index': 11, 'chunk_metadata': {'strategy': 'semantic', 'type': 'ContentType.TEXT', 'headings': [], 'page_number': [1], 'extra': None}}
