2025-07-11 16:50:40 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:164 - 创建分块 8: 数据键 = ['id', 'document_id', 'content', 'chunk_index', 'chunk_metadata']
2025-07-11 16:50:40 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:165 - 创建分块 8: 数据 = {'id': '403b7748-5209-4c6e-bd34-d34f7b2aed65', 'document_id': 'a5cac770-d5bb-47d3-b0b4-b679c08dacb0', 'content': 'the gamma function: $$ {\\sigma }_{f} = {\\sigma }_{0}\\sqrt{\\Gamma \\left( {1 + \\frac{1}{m}}\\right) } \\tag{6} $$ The standard deviation, $S$ , was calculated using the following equation: $$ S = {\\sigma }_{0}\\sqrt{\\Gamma \\left( {1 + \\frac{2}{m}}\\right) - {\\left\\lbrack \\Gamma \\left( 1 + \\frac{1}{m}\\right) \\right\\rbrack }^{2}} \\tag{7} $$ ## Materials Characterization SEM micrographs of the structure of the bamboo-reinforced geopolymer composite bar (Fig. 3) showed gaps between the bamboo fibers (the grey areas in Fig. 3) and the geopolymer matrix (the light areas in Fig. 3). Crack propagation through the geopolymer matrix deflected around the fibers. These gaps and crack development were characteristics of a weak interface. This may be due to the roughness and shrinkage of the untreated bamboo fibers, and the large amount of starch in the parenchymal cells of the fibers (Fig. 4). Large displacements observed in all samples before the initial vertical crack at the central point. Central displacement continued until the fibers stretched to breaking, accompanied by some fiber pullout which is a toughening mechanism. This behavior was also reflected in the shape of the stress-strain curves (Fig. 5), showing very smooth behavior without sharp kinks. The samples showed graceful failure, unlike pure geopolymers that failed suddenly without any warning. Hence, addition of bamboo fibers provided fracture toughness to the composite.\n![01973e1a-dfe2-7052-97bf-a3fb626b5a97_5_155_547_990_374_0.jpg](images/01973e1a-dfe2-7052-97bf-a3fb626b5a97_5_155_547_990_374_0.jpg)\nFigure 3. SEM micrographs of bamboo-reinforced K-based geopolymer composite tested specimen, showing gaps and crack propagation in back scattered composite (BEC) mode, 230x and BEC mode, 150x.\n![01973e1a-dfe2-7052-97bf-a3fb626b5a97_5_156_1029_988_373_0.jpg](images/01973e1a-dfe2-7052-97bf-a3fb626b5a97_5_156_1029_988_373_0.jpg)', 'chunk_index': 8, 'chunk_metadata': {'strategy': 'semantic', 'type': 'ContentType.TEXT', 'headings': [], 'page_number': [1], 'extra': None}}
