2025-07-11 19:45:10 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:164 - 创建分块 4: 数据键 = ['id', 'document_id', 'content', 'chunk_index', 'chunk_metadata']
2025-07-11 19:45:10 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:165 - 创建分块 4: 数据 = {'id': '688b26c2-c9ad-416f-b57b-d68a4a80ab12', 'document_id': '7266f9ca-00f6-4557-a304-b15b6dfe1de1', 'content': ' Presently, we found no published work on metakaolin-based geopolymer reinforced with bamboo fibers. This is a preliminary research on geopolymer composites reinforced with bamboo fibers. The bamboo-reinforced geopolymer composites are designed as green, sustainable materials, alternative to conventional cements and concretes.\n## MATERIALS AND METHODS\n## Bamboo Fibers\nGuadua angustifolia bamboo species was selected based on abundance, accessibility, mechanical properties and durability. Four-year old bamboo culms were collected from a research plantation area ${}^{{21},{22}}$ at the Brazilian National Institute for Amazonian Research (INPA) in Manaus, Amazonas, Brazil, and immersed in water for three days for better machine workability. The green bamboo culms (Fig. 1(a)) were chopped through a chipper and the chips (Fig. 1(b)) were transformed into fiber bundles (Fig. 1(c)) through a hammer mill. The bamboo fiber bundles were air-dried and filtered by a sifter machine through a mesh #4(12.51 - 40mm), producing short bamboo fibers. The main chemical constituents of bamboo are ${60}\\%$ cellulose and ${32}\\%$ lignin and hemicellulose ${}^{23}$ . Minor constituents are resins, tannins, waxes, inorganic salts. At the Geopolymer Laboratory of the University of Illinois at Urbana-Champaign (UIUC), USA, before utilization the bamboo fibers were washed with deionized water and set to dry-in two layers of paper towels.\n![01973e1a-dfe2-7052-97bf-a3fb626b5a97_2_151_1414_987_193_0.jpg](images/01973e1a-dfe2-7052-97bf-a3fb626b5a97_2_151_1414_987_193_0.jpg)\nFigure 1. Green bamboo (a) culms, (b) chips and (c) fiber bundles.', 'chunk_index': 4, 'chunk_metadata': {'strategy': 'semantic', 'type': 'ContentType.TEXT', 'headings': [], 'page_number': [1], 'extra': None}}
2025-07-11 19:45:10 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:178 - 分块 4 创建成功: 688b26c2-c9ad-416f-b57b-d68a4a80ab12
2025-07-11 19:45:10 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:164 - 创建分块 5: 数据键 = ['id', 'document_id', 'content', 'chunk_index', 'chunk_metadata']
2025-07-11 19:45:10 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:165 - 创建分块 5: 数据 = {'id': '0839eb64-937c-4f66-a9e6-3aef4b2aa5c5', 'document_id': '7266f9ca-00f6-4557-a304-b15b6dfe1de1', 'content': 'resins, tannins, waxes, inorganic salts. At the Geopolymer Laboratory of the University of Illinois at Urbana-Champaign (UIUC), USA, before utilization the bamboo fibers were washed with deionized water and set to dry-in two layers of paper towels. ![01973e1a-dfe2-7052-97bf-a3fb626b5a97_2_151_1414_987_193_0.jpg](images/01973e1a-dfe2-7052-97bf-a3fb626b5a97_2_151_1414_987_193_0.jpg) Figure 1. Green bamboo (a) culms, (b) chips and (c) fiber bundles. ## Geopolymer Composite\nPotassium silicate solution (or potassium water glass) was made by mixing fumed silica (Cab-O-Sil EH-5 Cabot Corp, Tuscola, IL) with potassium hydroxide pellets (Fisher Scientific Waltham, MA, USA) dissolved in deionized water according to the reaction:\n$$ 2\\mathrm{{KOH}} + {10}{\\mathrm{H}}_{2}0 + 2{\\mathrm{{SiO}}}_{2} \\rightarrow {\\mathrm{K}}_{2}\\mathrm{O} \\cdot 2{\\mathrm{{SiO}}}_{2} \\cdot {11}{\\mathrm{H}}_{2}\\mathrm{O} \\tag{1} $$\nGeopolymer was prepared at the Geopolymer Laboratory by mixing potassium water glass and Metamax ${}^{\\circledR }$ metakaolin $\\left( {{\\mathrm{{Al}}}_{2}{\\mathrm{O}}_{3} \\cdot 2{\\mathrm{{SiO}}}_{2}}\\right)$ (particle size ${1.3\\mu }\\mathrm{m}$ ) mixed with a high shear mixer for 5 minutes at ${1100}\\mathrm{{rpm}}$ to obtain good mixing of the components. This slurry was then put in a planetary conditioning mixer that further mixed and degassed for removal of fine bubbles. The geopolymerization reaction can be summed up as follows:\n## ${\\mathrm{K}}_{2}\\mathrm{O} \\cdot 2{\\mathrm{{SiO}}}_{2} \\cdot {11}{\\mathrm{H}}_{2}\\mathrm{O} + 2{\\mathrm{{SiO}}}_{2} \\cdot {\\mathrm{{Al}}}_{2}{\\mathrm{O}}_{3} \\rightarrow {\\mathrm{K}}_{2}\\mathrm{O} \\cdot {\\mathrm{{Al}}}_{2}{\\mathrm{O}}_{3} \\cdot 4{\\mathrm{{SiO}}}_{2} \\cdot {11}{\\mathrm{H}}_{2}\\mathrm{O}$\n(2)', 'chunk_index': 5, 'chunk_metadata': {'strategy': 'semantic', 'type': 'ContentType.TEXT', 'headings': [], 'page_number': [1], 'extra': None}}
2025-07-11 19:45:10 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:178 - 分块 5 创建成功: 0839eb64-937c-4f66-a9e6-3aef4b2aa5c5
2025-07-11 19:45:10 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:164 - 创建分块 6: 数据键 = ['id', 'document_id', 'content', 'chunk_index', 'chunk_metadata']
2025-07-11 19:45:10 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:165 - 创建分块 6: 数据 = {'id': '30e72b8a-85c4-4368-be0d-6ac936c73b7a', 'document_id': '7266f9ca-00f6-4557-a304-b15b6dfe1de1', 'content': 'the components. This slurry was then put in a planetary conditioning mixer that further mixed and degassed for removal of fine bubbles. The geopolymerization reaction can be summed up as follows: ## ${\\mathrm{K}}_{2}\\mathrm{O} \\cdot 2{\\mathrm{{SiO}}}_{2} \\cdot {11}{\\mathrm{H}}_{2}\\mathrm{O} + 2{\\mathrm{{SiO}}}_{2} \\cdot {\\mathrm{{Al}}}_{2}{\\mathrm{O}}_{3} \\rightarrow {\\mathrm{K}}_{2}\\mathrm{O} \\cdot {\\mathrm{{Al}}}_{2}{\\mathrm{O}}_{3} \\cdot 4{\\mathrm{{SiO}}}_{2} \\cdot {11}{\\mathrm{H}}_{2}\\mathrm{O}$ (2) Seven ( ${155} \\times {25} \\times 4\\mathrm{\\;{mm}}$ ) bamboo-reinforced geopolymer composite samples (Fig. 2(b)) were subjected to third-point loading flexural strength testing according to ASTM standard C1341- ${13}^{24}$ . The average density of bamboo-reinforced geopolymer composites was ${1.35}\\mathrm{\\;g}{\\mathrm{\\;{cm}}}^{-3}$ . Tests were carried out by an Instron testing machine at the Advanced Mechanical Testing and Evaluation Laboratory (AMTEL) of UIUC. The test span to depth ratio was 25:1, and the crosshead displacement rate was ${0.10}\\mathrm{\\;{mm}}{\\mathrm{\\;s}}^{-1}$ . Strain was measured by crosshead displacement.\n![01973e1a-dfe2-7052-97bf-a3fb626b5a97_3_206_1229_888_245_0.jpg](images/01973e1a-dfe2-7052-97bf-a3fb626b5a97_3_206_1229_888_245_0.jpg)\nFigure 2. Bamboo-reinforced geopolymer composite (a) plate and (b) tested specimens.\nWeibull functions were calculated using a linear regression method. A distribution function, $\\mathrm{F}$ , was estimated based on the flexure strength of $\\mathrm{n}$ samples using the median rank method shown in the following equation:\n$$ F \\cong \\frac{i - {0.3}}{n + {0.4}} \\tag{3} $$\nwhere the flexure strength for each sample is given a rank $i$ from 1 to $\\mathrm{n}$ , which is the number of samples in the batch. The Weibull distribution function is given by equation (4), where $\\mathrm{v}$ is the non-dimensional volume, $\\sigma$ is the stress, ${\\sigma }_{0}$ is the scale parameter and $m$ is the shape parameter or Weibull modulus:', 'chunk_index': 6, 'chunk_metadata': {'strategy': 'semantic', 'type': 'ContentType.TEXT', 'headings': [], 'page_number': [1], 'extra': None}}
