2025-07-11 21:33:15 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:164 - 创建分块 0: 数据键 = ['id', 'document_id', 'content', 'chunk_index', 'chunk_metadata']
2025-07-11 21:33:15 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:165 - 创建分块 0: 数据 = {'id': '25107ba7-836c-47a3-add8-22f931deb817', 'document_id': '0598c657-0b7a-4c72-b91d-55f30607886e', 'content': 'Geopolymer refers to a large group of nanoporous, nanoparticulate materials that are synthesized by dissolution and polycondensation of aluminosilicates in basic solutions and can be made from a variety of starting materials, such as industrial waste ash, volcanic rock, or calcined clay. Geopolymers are X-ray amorphous, corrosion resistant, refractory, and made at ambient temperature and pressure similar to cements. In this study, potassium metakaolin-based geopolymer (KGP) composites containing alumina platelets and glass frit were fabricated, and the impact of heating temperature, dwell time, and heating/cooling rate on the microstructure was studied. The composites, heat treated up to ${900}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ for up to ${20}\\mathrm{\\;h}$ using heating/cooling rates of up to ${1}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}/\\mathrm{{min}}$ , showed that the addition of alumina platelets prevented major microcracking and was also able to reduce linear shrinkage. Glass frit has been shown to heal microcracks formed during KGP dehydration and crystallization. The resulting material had an open porosity of less than $1\\%$ and a uniform surface glaze of ${250\\mu }\\mathrm{m}$ thickness, while Oswald ripening of round closed pores occurred due to the migration of molten glass in the system.\n## KEYWORDS\nalumina, geopolymer, glass frit, heat treatment, porosity, potassium\n## 1 INTRODUCTION', 'chunk_index': 0, 'chunk_metadata': {'strategy': 'semantic', 'type': 'ContentType.TEXT', 'headings': [], 'page_number': [1], 'extra': {'paragraph_index': 12, 'original_length': 1286}}}
2025-07-11 21:33:15 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:178 - 分块 0 创建成功: 25107ba7-836c-47a3-add8-22f931deb817
2025-07-11 21:33:15 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:164 - 创建分块 1: 数据键 = ['id', 'document_id', 'content', 'chunk_index', 'chunk_metadata']
2025-07-11 21:33:15 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:165 - 创建分块 1: 数据 = {'id': '116ab151-5f7a-411b-ab2b-cdaf3426bec3', 'document_id': '0598c657-0b7a-4c72-b91d-55f30607886e', 'content': 'resulting material had an open porosity of less than $1\\%$ and a uniform surface glaze of ${250\\mu }\\mathrm{m}$ thickness, while Oswald ripening of round closed pores occurred due to the migration of molten glass in the system. ## KEYWORDS alumina, geopolymer, glass frit, heat treatment, porosity, potassium ## 1 INTRODUCTION Geopolymer refers to a large group of nanoporous, nanoparticulate materials that are synthesized by dissolution and polycondensation of aluminosilicates in basic solutions and can be made from a variety of starting materials, such as industrial waste ash, volcanic rock, or calcined clay. ${}^{1 - 9}$ Geopolymers are a castable refractory, meaning complex molds and shapes can be filled and cured at room temperature in as little as ${24}\\mathrm{\\;h}$ and subsequently used at high temperature. ${}^{10}$ Geopolymers are X-ray amorphous, corrosion resistant, refractory, and made at ambient temperature and pressure in a manner similar to cements. ${}^{{11} - {15}}$ As such, geopolymers have been compared extensively to ordinary Portland cement (OPC) for use in civil and structural engineering. ${}^{{16} - {19}}$ For example, it has been shown that geopolymers perform better than OPC in saline environments. ${}^{20}$ This was found to be due to the geopolymers preferentially forming covalently bonded networks of aluminosilicates rather than calcium silicate hydrates, resulting in enhanced resistance to alkali leaching.\n---\nThis is an open access article under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution License, which permits use, distribution and reproduction in any medium, provided the original work is properly cited.\n© 2022 The Authors. International Journal of Ceramic Engineering & Science published by Wiley Periodicals LLC. on behalf of the American Ceramic Society.\n---', 'chunk_index': 1, 'chunk_metadata': {'strategy': 'semantic', 'type': 'ContentType.TEXT', 'headings': [], 'page_number': [1], 'extra': None}}
2025-07-11 21:33:15 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:178 - 分块 1 创建成功: 116ab151-5f7a-411b-ab2b-cdaf3426bec3
2025-07-11 21:33:15 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:164 - 创建分块 2: 数据键 = ['id', 'document_id', 'content', 'chunk_index', 'chunk_metadata']
2025-07-11 21:33:15 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:165 - 创建分块 2: 数据 = {'id': 'f3729449-0cc3-4e22-af4d-e803ec2ebbf0', 'document_id': '0598c657-0b7a-4c72-b91d-55f30607886e', 'content': 'article under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution License, which permits use, distribution and reproduction in any medium, provided the original work is properly cited. © 2022 The Authors. International Journal of Ceramic Engineering & Science published by Wiley Periodicals LLC. on behalf of the American Ceramic Society. --- The prevention of microcracking in heat-treated geopolymer composites has been an area of interest because material impermeability and higher mechanical strengths could be achieved if this microcracking was eliminated or controlled. One successful strategy that has been identified to minimize microcracking is the use of glass-forming materials dispersed in a geopolymer composite. ${}^{{28},{29}}$ The glass phase melts and permeates microcracks when exposed to high temperature, effectively filling the microcracks during the sintering process. This forms a material that is effectively "self-healing" because any additional cracks that develop could be filled by the glassy phase upon reheating. Although the inclusion of a glass-forming phase has been shown in these two papers to be beneficial, it is not yet known if the glassy phase separates uniformly or not. There has also been little investigation into different glass percentages and compositions, different alumina morphologies, and the effect of specimen size on the cracking characteristics. Therefore, the current study aims to investigate a composite of geopolymer, alumina platelets, and glass powder. The uniformity of deformation that occurs during heating will be studied, along with the effect of specimen size on micro- and macro-cracking behaviors, as well as the self-healing properties of the composite.\n## 2 | METHODOLOGY\n### 2.1 | Material synthesis', 'chunk_index': 2, 'chunk_metadata': {'strategy': 'semantic', 'type': 'ContentType.TEXT', 'headings': [], 'page_number': [1], 'extra': None}}
2025-07-11 21:33:15 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:178 - 分块 2 创建成功: f3729449-0cc3-4e22-af4d-e803ec2ebbf0
