2025-07-11 21:33:15 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:165 - 创建分块 11: 数据 = {'id': 'ccd6de82-d24a-4ca8-9bfb-6a8e549328ea', 'document_id': '0598c657-0b7a-4c72-b91d-55f30607886e', 'content': 'heated for 5and ${20}\\mathrm{\\;h}$ . ![0197448d-ce32-7a6e-bb80-13bab7cf1e87_6_340_135_1092_410_0.jpg](images/0197448d-ce32-7a6e-bb80-13bab7cf1e87_6_340_135_1092_410_0.jpg) FIG URE 12 Alumina platelets (left) and glass powder (right) before addition to the potassium metakaolin-based geopolymer (KGP) matrix ![0197448d-ce32-7a6e-bb80-13bab7cf1e87_6_342_713_1090_427_0.jpg](images/0197448d-ce32-7a6e-bb80-13bab7cf1e87_6_342_713_1090_427_0.jpg) FIG URE 13 Geopolymer composite before (left) and after heating to ${900}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ for ${20}\\mathrm{\\;h}$ using heating/cooling rates of ${1}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}/\\mathrm{{min}}$ (right) For those samples heated to ${600}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ and below, discrete glass and alumina particles could be seen in the microstructure. The alumina particles could be identified by the darker shade of gray compared to the glass particles. The geopolymer matrix between particles, however, became more cracked with increasing temperature. After sintering at ${900}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ , a significant change in microstructure occurred. Cracking was no longer present, indicating that liquid glass permeated cracks formed by dehydration and crystallization. Inert alumina identified by its weaker electron signal could still be seen in the microstructure after heat treatment at ${900}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ and did not change shape, implying that no chemical reaction had occurred. The boundaries between the glass and matrix after heating to ${900}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ became globular compared to their angular appearance after lower temperature treatments. Samples heated to ${300}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ and ${600}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ did not have circular pores, as shown in Figure 14\nPolished cross-sections of specimens heated to ${900}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ were inspected using EDS, as shown in Figure 15. Three distinct phases could be confirmed. The highest aluminium phase was alumina platelets, the phase highest in $\\mathrm{K}$ was leucite, and the third phase with medium amounts of aluminium and potassium was amorphous glass.', 'chunk_index': 11, 'chunk_metadata': {'strategy': 'semantic', 'type': 'ContentType.TEXT', 'headings': [], 'page_number': [1], 'extra': None}}
