2025-07-11 21:33:15 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:164 - 创建分块 14: 数据键 = ['id', 'document_id', 'content', 'chunk_index', 'chunk_metadata']
2025-07-11 21:33:15 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:165 - 创建分块 14: 数据 = {'id': '4ed9a24b-9eea-4cad-86a0-0685d4279078', 'document_id': '0598c657-0b7a-4c72-b91d-55f30607886e', 'content': 'the glass phase, from the geopolymer, or from a reaction between one of the phases present. In order to determine the origin of the leucite, individual phases were heated to ${900}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ for ${20}\\mathrm{\\;h}$ , and the XRD results were compared, as shown in Figures 18 and 19. ![0197448d-ce32-7a6e-bb80-13bab7cf1e87_8_129_134_734_620_0.jpg](images/0197448d-ce32-7a6e-bb80-13bab7cf1e87_8_129_134_734_620_0.jpg) FIGURE 16 Polished cross-section of amorphous self-healing geopolymer (ASH-G) heated to ${900}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ for ${20}\\mathrm{\\;h}$ . A uniform glaze of $\\sim {250\\mu }\\mathrm{m}$ formed on free surfaces\nFigure 19 shows that the glass frit used in this material remained amorphous after extended annealing at ${900}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ . However, the plain KGP matrix without alumina or glass particles revealed the formation of leucite at ${900}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ , with the peak locations corresponding to those found in the composite materials after heating to ${900}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ . This confirmed that it was the geopolymer matrix that transformed into leucite during heating. These results also indicated that no chemical reaction occurred between the three phases in the ASH-G composite\n## 4 | DISCUSSION\n### 4.1 | Reinforcement phase\nThe addition of a reinforcing phase into a geopolymer is well known to increase mechanical properties both before and after exposure to elevated temperatures. In the current study, reinforcement was achieved through the addition of alumina platelets, while the glass particulates acted as a reinforcement up to ${600}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ and then as a healing phase after heating to ${900}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ . This was due to glass particulate melting and healing microcracks resulting from geopolymer dehydration.\n### 4.2 | Microstructure development', 'chunk_index': 14, 'chunk_metadata': {'strategy': 'semantic', 'type': 'ContentType.TEXT', 'headings': [], 'page_number': [1], 'extra': None}}
