2025-07-11 21:33:15 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:164 - 创建分块 15: 数据键 = ['id', 'document_id', 'content', 'chunk_index', 'chunk_metadata']
2025-07-11 21:33:15 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:165 - 创建分块 15: 数据 = {'id': '00a1ea33-74f6-41a1-8f32-e558f5265a87', 'document_id': '0598c657-0b7a-4c72-b91d-55f30607886e', 'content': 'the addition of alumina platelets, while the glass particulates acted as a reinforcement up to ${600}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ and then as a healing phase after heating to ${900}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ . This was due to glass particulate melting and healing microcracks resulting from geopolymer dehydration. ### 4.2 | Microstructure development Data from STA of the glass particulates revealed a change in heat flow and peak heat flow at ${542}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ and ${573}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ , respectively, as seen in Figure 10. These values can be interpreted as corresponding to the glass softening and melting temperatures. The glass manufacturer marketed the glass as being fusible between ${730}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ and ${800}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ and, as such, quoted forming ranges that were not scientifically well defined. ${}^{37}$ The difference between STA and the manufacturer recommendation was presumed to be due to the temperature dependence of glass viscosity. ${}^{38}$ Although the glass particulates had a melting peak less than ${600}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ , it was likely that at this temperature, the glass was so viscous that it did not flow during heat treatment, retaining its size and shape, as seen in Figure 14. Only after heating to ${900}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ did the glass appear to melt and flow into the microcracks within the microstructure, as seen in Figure 14. The ability to flow into open space and fill cracks is considered a self-healing property, and the present results indicate that within the composite studied here, the temperature must be well beyond the softening temperature of the glass to create the self-healing behavior. The temperature must have been sufficiently high that the glass developed fluid-like properties, which allowed it to flow into the small voids within the microstructure.', 'chunk_index': 15, 'chunk_metadata': {'strategy': 'semantic', 'type': 'ContentType.TEXT', 'headings': [], 'page_number': [1], 'extra': None}}
