2025-07-11 21:33:15 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:164 - 创建分块 16: 数据键 = ['id', 'document_id', 'content', 'chunk_index', 'chunk_metadata']
2025-07-11 21:33:15 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:165 - 创建分块 16: 数据 = {'id': 'd7311fed-fbed-4eb0-827e-9dbfe8626caf', 'document_id': '0598c657-0b7a-4c72-b91d-55f30607886e', 'content': 'results indicate that within the composite studied here, the temperature must be well beyond the softening temperature of the glass to create the self-healing behavior. The temperature must have been sufficiently high that the glass developed fluid-like properties, which allowed it to flow into the small voids within the microstructure. The geopolymer composite experienced the most weight loss of ${10}\\%$ after heating to ${300}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ for $5\\mathrm{\\;h}$ according to macroscopic measurements, as shown in Figure 7. TGA in Figure 10 confirmed that the highest amount of weight loss occurred after ${200}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ , and this mass loss was attributed to the removal of bound water in the KGP, which has been observed before in these materials. ${}^{39}$ The $5\\mathrm{{wt}}\\%$ difference between the TGA and macroscopic measurements is attributed to the small sample size of the TGA and perhaps the vacuum-nitrogen purge, which deposited inert nitrogen into the micropores.\nThe measured shrinkage values correlated well with the measured increase in density of the composite with increasing temperature. The maximum shrinkage of $8\\%$ after heating to ${900}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ was consistent with the increase in density of ${20}\\%$ .\nThe reduced linear shrinkage of ASH-G accompanied by mass loss at ${300}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ was attributed to the number of reinforcing phases, as suggested by Figure 6. The density of the geopolymer composite increased by a minor amount after heating to ${600}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ , after which the glassy phase began to melt and fill microcracks, thereby drastically increasing the density. The filling of microcracks by the molten glass after heating to ${900}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ eliminated open porosity, as seen in Figure 9.', 'chunk_index': 16, 'chunk_metadata': {'strategy': 'semantic', 'type': 'ContentType.TEXT', 'headings': [], 'page_number': [1], 'extra': None}}
