2025-07-11 21:33:15 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:164 - 创建分块 18: 数据键 = ['id', 'document_id', 'content', 'chunk_index', 'chunk_metadata']
2025-07-11 21:33:15 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:165 - 创建分块 18: 数据 = {'id': 'c465eae3-448f-45b5-a6cd-aadc4ba18278', 'document_id': '0598c657-0b7a-4c72-b91d-55f30607886e', 'content': 'to ${900}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ showed that the glass could repeatedly melt and fill cracks upon heating to ${900}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ . Similarly, the glass glaze can act as a uniform fusing base that can be used to join multiple pieces using localized heat, that is, acetylene torch, infrared heat, etc. The cause of hairline cracking of large diameter(40mm) samples after heating to ${600}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ and ${900}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ could be due not only to the shrinkage of the KGP, which comprises 58 vol% after curing but also the glass phase losing structural strength. This is because the glass phase begins to melt at ${573}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ according to Figure 10 but does not flow to produce the glaze seen in Figure 16. The direction of cracking was always vertical, implying that cracking was related to hoop stressing, which is proportional to the radius.\n## 5 | CONCLUSIONS\nIn this study, KGP composites containing alumina platelets and glass frit were studied. The composites were heated to various temperatures in order to examine their physical and microstructural changes. The following conclusions can be drawn:\n1. The inclusion of alumina platelets and glass powder into KGP produced an ASH-G composite after slow heating at ${1}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}/\\mathrm{{min}}$ to ${900}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ .\n2. The open porosity was reduced to less than 1%, while the density increased by 17%. Additionally, heat treatment to ${900}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ increased the closed porosity to ${10}\\%$ .\n3. A uniform glaze of ${250\\mu }\\mathrm{m}$ formed on the surface of the samples but only when heated to ${900}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ .\n4. No reaction occurred among the three phases, indicating the repeatability of glass melting, filling cracks, and acting as a fusing material.', 'chunk_index': 18, 'chunk_metadata': {'strategy': 'semantic', 'type': 'ContentType.TEXT', 'headings': [], 'page_number': [1], 'extra': None}}
