2025-07-11 21:33:37 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:165 - 创建分块 8: 数据 = {'id': '69e1216f-22ca-4eba-9d3a-626e5fe23509', 'document_id': '846eec7c-7c34-46ba-806b-aa960ee98a23', 'content': 'Chemical Co., Ltd. Samut Prakan, Thailand</td></tr><tr><td>Fly ash</td><td>By-product</td><td>Mae Moh power station, Lampang, Thailand</td></tr><tr><td>Sodium hydroxide</td><td>Analytical</td><td>Loba Chemie Pvt. Ltd. Mumbai, India</td></tr><tr><td>Sodium silicate solution</td><td>Commercial</td><td>Luoyang Qihang Chemical Industrial Co., Ltd, Luoyang, China</td></tr></table> TABLE 2 Formulations and mixing schedule for preparation of NR/geopolymer composites <table><tr><td>Ingredients</td><td>Quantities (phr)</td><td>Mixing time (min)</td></tr><tr><td>NR</td><td>100</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>ZnO</td><td>5</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>Stearic acid</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>Geopolymer</td><td>0,10,20,30and 40</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>MBTs</td><td>0.5</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>Sulfur</td><td>2.5</td><td>2</td></tr></table> ### 2.2 | Preparation of geopolymers Fly ash was first mixed with ${10}\\mathrm{M}\\mathrm{{NaOH}}$ solution in a Hobart mixer for about $2\\mathrm{\\;{min}}$ , with a paddle speed of approximately ${285}\\mathrm{{rpm}}$ . Then, the ${\\mathrm{{Na}}}_{2}{\\mathrm{{SiO}}}_{3}$ solution was added, and mixing was continued for another 2 min. The mixture was then immediately cast into a mold and conditioned at room temperature for at least $4\\mathrm{\\;{hr}}$ as the final setting time. It was then annealed by conditioning in a hot air oven at approximately ${65}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ for $4\\mathrm{{hr}}$ and then further annealed in a microwave at ${450}\\mathrm{\\;W}$ for $5\\mathrm{\\;{min}}$ . Finally, the geopolymer product was ground in a ball mill for about ${48}\\mathrm{{hr}}$ . Then, the geopolymer powder was sieved through a 200-mesh screen before compounding with NR. The functional groups in the geopolymer particles were characterized by Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR, Vertex70, Bruker, Karlsruhe, Germany). Also, the particle size distribution of geopolymer was analyzed by a particle size analyzer (Beckman Coulter LS 230, Vernon Hills, IL, USA). The morphology of geopolymer particles was also imaged by scanning electron microscopy (SEM, FEI-Quanta 400, Jeol Ltd., Tokyo, Japan). Furthermore, the density of geopolymer was gravimetrically measured, and its $\\mathrm{{pH}}$ was determined by the procedure described in ISO 10523: 1994.\n### 2.3 | Analysis of FA and GP', 'chunk_index': 8, 'chunk_metadata': {'strategy': 'semantic', 'type': 'ContentType.TEXT', 'headings': [], 'page_number': [1], 'extra': None}}
