2025-07-11 21:33:37 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:165 - 创建分块 27: 数据 = {'id': '64f553b7-843b-4d6f-a95f-87e9b7007f3d', 'document_id': '846eec7c-7c34-46ba-806b-aa960ee98a23', 'content': 'loss, residual mass at ${550}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ and degradation temperature $\\left( {T}_{\\mathrm{d}}\\right)$ of gum, GP- and FA (10 phr)-filled NR vulcanizates <table><tr><td rowspan="2">Samples</td><td colspan="4">Decomposition temperature at different $\\%$ weight loss</td><td rowspan="2">Residual (%) mass at ${550}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$</td><td rowspan="2">Degradation temperature (℃)</td></tr><tr><td>${\\mathbf{T}}_{5\\% }$</td><td>${T}_{{10}\\% }$</td><td>${T}_{{50}\\% }$</td><td>${T}_{{80}\\% }$</td></tr><tr><td>Gum NR</td><td>334.3</td><td>349.8</td><td>385.3</td><td>423.6</td><td>5.9</td><td>375.7</td></tr><tr><td>NR-GP10</td><td>342.8</td><td>352.5</td><td>386.1</td><td>440.2</td><td>16.7</td><td>376.5</td></tr><tr><td>NR-FA10</td><td>339.9</td><td>351.9</td><td>387.6</td><td>436.7</td><td>14.3</td><td>372.8</td></tr></table> ### 3.7 | Thermal properties Thermogravimetric analysis measures the amount and rate of mass change in a sample as functions of temperature or time in a controlled atmosphere. The measurement is used primarily to determine the thermal and oxidative stabilities of materials as well as their compositional properties. Figure 12 shows TGA and DTG thermograms of the gum NR, and the GP- or FA (10phr)-filled NR vulcanizates. The observed weight loss, residual mass at ${550}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ , and degradation temperature $\\left( {T}_{\\mathrm{d}}\\right)$ are summarized in Table 7. It is clear that the thermal stability of GP-filled NR composites $\\left( {{T}_{\\mathrm{d}} = {376.5}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}}\\right)$ was slightly improved from that of the gum $\\mathrm{{NR}}\\left( {{T}_{\\mathrm{d}} = {375.7}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}}\\right)$ by the filler-rubber interactions. Moreover, it is seen the NR/FA composites had the poorest thermal stability $\\left( {{T}_{\\mathrm{d}} = {372.8}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}}\\right)$ , which might be attributed to the metal oxide impurities in FA (Table 4) that could facilitate thermal degradation of the polymer networks. ${}^{\\left\\lbrack {11}\\right\\rbrack }$ In Figure 12, it is seen that the first degradation step of the GP- or FA-filled NR composites started at about ${330}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ and was completed around ${450}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ , and in this temperature range, the degradation of natural rubber (polyisoprene) component is dominant giving the major peaks of the DTG curves. The second degradation stage occurred in the temperature range from 450 to ${600}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ , where the natural rubber component degrades in the presence of oxidized structures as well as by depletion of sulphidic cross-links.', 'chunk_index': 27, 'chunk_metadata': {'strategy': 'semantic', 'type': 'ContentType.TEXT', 'headings': [], 'page_number': [1], 'extra': None}}
