2025-07-11 21:46:09 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:175 - 创建分块 1: 数据键 = ['id', 'document_id', 'content', 'chunk_index', 'chunk_metadata']
2025-07-11 21:46:09 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:176 - 创建分块 1: 数据 = {'id': '5eb9753e-1ad9-4d3c-a5e4-dbe66b9b38ab', 'document_id': 'b183cc30-9d9a-4baa-9df9-70314ed01c98', 'content': 'resulting material had an open porosity of less than $1\\%$ and a uniform surface glaze of ${250\\mu }\\mathrm{m}$ thickness, while Oswald ripening of round closed pores occurred due to the migration of molten glass in the system. ## KEYWORDS alumina, geopolymer, glass frit, heat treatment, porosity, potassium ## 1 INTRODUCTION Geopolymer refers to a large group of nanoporous, nanoparticulate materials that are synthesized by dissolution and polycondensation of aluminosilicates in basic solutions and can be made from a variety of starting materials, such as industrial waste ash, volcanic rock, or calcined clay. ${}^{1 - 9}$ Geopolymers are a castable refractory, meaning complex molds and shapes can be filled and cured at room temperature in as little as ${24}\\mathrm{\\;h}$ and subsequently used at high temperature. ${}^{10}$ Geopolymers are X-ray amorphous, corrosion resistant, refractory, and made at ambient temperature and pressure in a manner similar to cements. ${}^{{11} - {15}}$ As such, geopolymers have been compared extensively to ordinary Portland cement (OPC) for use in civil and structural engineering. ${}^{{16} - {19}}$ For example, it has been shown that geopolymers perform better than OPC in saline environments. ${}^{20}$ This was found to be due to the geopolymers preferentially forming covalently bonded networks of aluminosilicates rather than calcium silicate hydrates, resulting in enhanced resistance to alkali leaching.\n---\nThis is an open access article under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution License, which permits use, distribution and reproduction in any medium, provided the original work is properly cited.\n© 2022 The Authors. International Journal of Ceramic Engineering & Science published by Wiley Periodicals LLC. on behalf of the American Ceramic Society.\n---', 'chunk_index': 1, 'chunk_metadata': {'strategy': 'semantic', 'type': 'ContentType.TEXT', 'headings': [], 'page_number': [1], 'extra': None}}
