2025-07-11 21:46:09 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:175 - 创建分块 14: 数据键 = ['id', 'document_id', 'content', 'chunk_index', 'chunk_metadata']
2025-07-11 21:46:09 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:176 - 创建分块 14: 数据 = {'id': 'b6886a16-ab9b-4070-8181-100d019a5799', 'document_id': 'b183cc30-9d9a-4baa-9df9-70314ed01c98', 'content': 'the glass phase, from the geopolymer, or from a reaction between one of the phases present. In order to determine the origin of the leucite, individual phases were heated to ${900}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ for ${20}\\mathrm{\\;h}$ , and the XRD results were compared, as shown in Figures 18 and 19. ![0197448d-ce32-7a6e-bb80-13bab7cf1e87_8_129_134_734_620_0.jpg](images/0197448d-ce32-7a6e-bb80-13bab7cf1e87_8_129_134_734_620_0.jpg) FIGURE 16 Polished cross-section of amorphous self-healing geopolymer (ASH-G) heated to ${900}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ for ${20}\\mathrm{\\;h}$ . A uniform glaze of $\\sim {250\\mu }\\mathrm{m}$ formed on free surfaces\nFigure 19 shows that the glass frit used in this material remained amorphous after extended annealing at ${900}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ . However, the plain KGP matrix without alumina or glass particles revealed the formation of leucite at ${900}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ , with the peak locations corresponding to those found in the composite materials after heating to ${900}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ . This confirmed that it was the geopolymer matrix that transformed into leucite during heating. These results also indicated that no chemical reaction occurred between the three phases in the ASH-G composite\n## 4 | DISCUSSION\n### 4.1 | Reinforcement phase\nThe addition of a reinforcing phase into a geopolymer is well known to increase mechanical properties both before and after exposure to elevated temperatures. In the current study, reinforcement was achieved through the addition of alumina platelets, while the glass particulates acted as a reinforcement up to ${600}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ and then as a healing phase after heating to ${900}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ . This was due to glass particulate melting and healing microcracks resulting from geopolymer dehydration.\n### 4.2 | Microstructure development', 'chunk_index': 14, 'chunk_metadata': {'strategy': 'semantic', 'type': 'ContentType.TEXT', 'headings': [], 'page_number': [1], 'extra': None}}
2025-07-11 21:46:09 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:189 - 分块 14 创建成功: b6886a16-ab9b-4070-8181-100d019a5799
2025-07-11 21:46:09 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:175 - 创建分块 15: 数据键 = ['id', 'document_id', 'content', 'chunk_index', 'chunk_metadata']
2025-07-11 21:46:09 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:176 - 创建分块 15: 数据 = {'id': '4bc58eea-2c04-409f-bff4-970b9368e364', 'document_id': 'b183cc30-9d9a-4baa-9df9-70314ed01c98', 'content': 'the addition of alumina platelets, while the glass particulates acted as a reinforcement up to ${600}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ and then as a healing phase after heating to ${900}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ . This was due to glass particulate melting and healing microcracks resulting from geopolymer dehydration. ### 4.2 | Microstructure development Data from STA of the glass particulates revealed a change in heat flow and peak heat flow at ${542}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ and ${573}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ , respectively, as seen in Figure 10. These values can be interpreted as corresponding to the glass softening and melting temperatures. The glass manufacturer marketed the glass as being fusible between ${730}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ and ${800}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ and, as such, quoted forming ranges that were not scientifically well defined. ${}^{37}$ The difference between STA and the manufacturer recommendation was presumed to be due to the temperature dependence of glass viscosity. ${}^{38}$ Although the glass particulates had a melting peak less than ${600}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ , it was likely that at this temperature, the glass was so viscous that it did not flow during heat treatment, retaining its size and shape, as seen in Figure 14. Only after heating to ${900}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ did the glass appear to melt and flow into the microcracks within the microstructure, as seen in Figure 14. The ability to flow into open space and fill cracks is considered a self-healing property, and the present results indicate that within the composite studied here, the temperature must be well beyond the softening temperature of the glass to create the self-healing behavior. The temperature must have been sufficiently high that the glass developed fluid-like properties, which allowed it to flow into the small voids within the microstructure.', 'chunk_index': 15, 'chunk_metadata': {'strategy': 'semantic', 'type': 'ContentType.TEXT', 'headings': [], 'page_number': [1], 'extra': None}}
2025-07-11 21:46:09 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:189 - 分块 15 创建成功: 4bc58eea-2c04-409f-bff4-970b9368e364
2025-07-11 21:46:09 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:175 - 创建分块 16: 数据键 = ['id', 'document_id', 'content', 'chunk_index', 'chunk_metadata']
2025-07-11 21:46:09 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:176 - 创建分块 16: 数据 = {'id': 'bfca8d7b-e0af-4a6f-ae77-85ddcc951a40', 'document_id': 'b183cc30-9d9a-4baa-9df9-70314ed01c98', 'content': 'results indicate that within the composite studied here, the temperature must be well beyond the softening temperature of the glass to create the self-healing behavior. The temperature must have been sufficiently high that the glass developed fluid-like properties, which allowed it to flow into the small voids within the microstructure. The geopolymer composite experienced the most weight loss of ${10}\\%$ after heating to ${300}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ for $5\\mathrm{\\;h}$ according to macroscopic measurements, as shown in Figure 7. TGA in Figure 10 confirmed that the highest amount of weight loss occurred after ${200}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ , and this mass loss was attributed to the removal of bound water in the KGP, which has been observed before in these materials. ${}^{39}$ The $5\\mathrm{{wt}}\\%$ difference between the TGA and macroscopic measurements is attributed to the small sample size of the TGA and perhaps the vacuum-nitrogen purge, which deposited inert nitrogen into the micropores.\nThe measured shrinkage values correlated well with the measured increase in density of the composite with increasing temperature. The maximum shrinkage of $8\\%$ after heating to ${900}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ was consistent with the increase in density of ${20}\\%$ .\nThe reduced linear shrinkage of ASH-G accompanied by mass loss at ${300}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ was attributed to the number of reinforcing phases, as suggested by Figure 6. The density of the geopolymer composite increased by a minor amount after heating to ${600}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ , after which the glassy phase began to melt and fill microcracks, thereby drastically increasing the density. The filling of microcracks by the molten glass after heating to ${900}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ eliminated open porosity, as seen in Figure 9.', 'chunk_index': 16, 'chunk_metadata': {'strategy': 'semantic', 'type': 'ContentType.TEXT', 'headings': [], 'page_number': [1], 'extra': None}}
