2025-07-11 21:46:09 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:175 - 创建分块 17: 数据键 = ['id', 'document_id', 'content', 'chunk_index', 'chunk_metadata']
2025-07-11 21:46:09 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:176 - 创建分块 17: 数据 = {'id': 'ffc258ef-3a92-43ee-9622-a0c915286f42', 'document_id': 'b183cc30-9d9a-4baa-9df9-70314ed01c98', 'content': 'increased by a minor amount after heating to ${600}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ , after which the glassy phase began to melt and fill microcracks, thereby drastically increasing the density. The filling of microcracks by the molten glass after heating to ${900}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ eliminated open porosity, as seen in Figure 9. The change from an open nanoporous KGP matrix containing dense particulate phases to a dense glass-leucite matrix containing Oswalt ripened, and closed porosity occurred after slow heating to ${900}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ for extended periods. KGP experienced a shift from open porosity to semi-closed porosity after heating to ${850}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C} \\cdot {}^{40}$ The cumulative closed pore area of ASH-G heated to ${900}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ was ${10}\\%$ , as shown in Figure 13. The 10% closed porosity was speculated to be due to trapped gaseous species.\nThe XRD data in Figure 19 showed that the geopoly-mer matrix transformed to leucite when heated at ${900}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ for extended periods. It has been shown that leucite formation corresponds to a geopolymer linear shrinkage of ${15}\\% {.}^{39}$ This shrinkage may have imparted local pressure on the glass melt, promoting flow of the glass phase into the microcracks within the microstructure. The lack of crystallization in glass heated to ${900}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ showed that the glass could repeatedly melt and fill cracks upon heating to ${900}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ . Similarly, the glass glaze can act as a uniform fusing base that can be used to join multiple pieces using localized heat, that is, acetylene torch, infrared heat, etc.', 'chunk_index': 17, 'chunk_metadata': {'strategy': 'semantic', 'type': 'ContentType.TEXT', 'headings': [], 'page_number': [1], 'extra': None}}
2025-07-11 21:46:09 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:189 - 分块 17 创建成功: ffc258ef-3a92-43ee-9622-a0c915286f42
2025-07-11 21:46:09 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:175 - 创建分块 18: 数据键 = ['id', 'document_id', 'content', 'chunk_index', 'chunk_metadata']
2025-07-11 21:46:09 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:176 - 创建分块 18: 数据 = {'id': '864cd291-049c-44bc-b2be-05e5b12e8c9c', 'document_id': 'b183cc30-9d9a-4baa-9df9-70314ed01c98', 'content': 'to ${900}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ showed that the glass could repeatedly melt and fill cracks upon heating to ${900}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ . Similarly, the glass glaze can act as a uniform fusing base that can be used to join multiple pieces using localized heat, that is, acetylene torch, infrared heat, etc. The cause of hairline cracking of large diameter(40mm) samples after heating to ${600}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ and ${900}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ could be due not only to the shrinkage of the KGP, which comprises 58 vol% after curing but also the glass phase losing structural strength. This is because the glass phase begins to melt at ${573}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ according to Figure 10 but does not flow to produce the glaze seen in Figure 16. The direction of cracking was always vertical, implying that cracking was related to hoop stressing, which is proportional to the radius.\n## 5 | CONCLUSIONS\nIn this study, KGP composites containing alumina platelets and glass frit were studied. The composites were heated to various temperatures in order to examine their physical and microstructural changes. The following conclusions can be drawn:\n1. The inclusion of alumina platelets and glass powder into KGP produced an ASH-G composite after slow heating at ${1}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}/\\mathrm{{min}}$ to ${900}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ .\n2. The open porosity was reduced to less than 1%, while the density increased by 17%. Additionally, heat treatment to ${900}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ increased the closed porosity to ${10}\\%$ .\n3. A uniform glaze of ${250\\mu }\\mathrm{m}$ formed on the surface of the samples but only when heated to ${900}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ .\n4. No reaction occurred among the three phases, indicating the repeatability of glass melting, filling cracks, and acting as a fusing material.', 'chunk_index': 18, 'chunk_metadata': {'strategy': 'semantic', 'type': 'ContentType.TEXT', 'headings': [], 'page_number': [1], 'extra': None}}
