2025-07-11 21:46:10 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:176 - 创建分块 3: 数据 = {'id': '60ebba72-671d-4bfb-a397-a6245e42c3c5', 'document_id': '7ff3822c-812a-43ee-8698-1aa9534912ec', 'content': 'diffraction analysis (Malvern Instrument Mastersizer, 2000) as presented in TABLE 2. In this study, a mixture of chemical-grade sodium hydroxide solution and commercial sodium silicate was used as the alkaline activator. ![01973e1a-dfde-72a9-84ae-b84e3d3bb8ca_2_212_1095_1193_410_0.jpg](images/01973e1a-dfde-72a9-84ae-b84e3d3bb8ca_2_212_1095_1193_410_0.jpg) Figure 1. SEM pictures of raw materials ![01973e1a-dfde-72a9-84ae-b84e3d3bb8ca_2_437_1593_766_507_0.jpg](images/01973e1a-dfde-72a9-84ae-b84e3d3bb8ca_2_437_1593_766_507_0.jpg) Figure 2. X-ray diffractogram of raw materials ## Sample Preparation The optimum concentration of sodium hydroxide solution in preparation of alkaline activator was predetermined by mixing various sodium hydroxide solutions ranging from 8-16 $\\mathrm{{mol}}/1$ with sodium silicate. Dry fly ash was mixed with alkaline activator and hand stirred for 1 minute. The mixture was then poured into cylindrical PVC molds ( $\\varnothing {15}\\mathrm{\\;{mm}} \\times {30}\\mathrm{\\;{mm}}$ ). The geopolymer-mullite composite was prepared by adding mullite powder, ranging from 20-60 wt% of dry fly ash, to the optimized geopolymer mixture followed by the same curing procedure. The detail of various mixtures of fly ash-based geopolymer and its composites with mullite is given in TABLE 3. The samples were cured at ${90}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ for 1 day and then at ${40}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ for 6 days.\n## Setting Time and Compressive Strength\nThe setting time of geopolymer paste was tested according to ASTM C-191 with a manually operated standard Vicat apparatus. The compressive strength was measured after 7 days of curing. The samples were demolded from cylindrical PVC molds, and subjected to the compressive test by using Universal Testing Machine (Instron 5566) with the loading speed of $1\\mathrm{\\;{mm}}/\\mathrm{{min}}$ . Each reported value is the average of five samples.\n## Scanning Electron Microscopy', 'chunk_index': 3, 'chunk_metadata': {'strategy': 'semantic', 'type': 'ContentType.TEXT', 'headings': [], 'page_number': [1], 'extra': None}}
2025-07-11 21:46:10 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:189 - 分块 3 创建成功: 60ebba72-671d-4bfb-a397-a6245e42c3c5
2025-07-11 21:46:10 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:175 - 创建分块 4: 数据键 = ['id', 'document_id', 'content', 'chunk_index', 'chunk_metadata']
2025-07-11 21:46:10 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:176 - 创建分块 4: 数据 = {'id': 'd838418f-c41d-4801-a8c0-e1708c3485ac', 'document_id': '7ff3822c-812a-43ee-8698-1aa9534912ec', 'content': "compressive strength was measured after 7 days of curing. The samples were demolded from cylindrical PVC molds, and subjected to the compressive test by using Universal Testing Machine (Instron 5566) with the loading speed of $1\\mathrm{\\;{mm}}/\\mathrm{{min}}$ . Each reported value is the average of five samples. ## Scanning Electron Microscopy Figure 7 presents the effect of $\\mathrm{{NaOH}}$ concentration on microstructures. It can be seen that at $8\\mathrm{\\;{mol}}/\\mathrm{l}$ of $\\mathrm{{NaOH}}$ concentration was insufficient to dissolve fly ash particle sincea large amount of unreacted particles still exist after polymerization. This is because strong alkalis are required to activate the silicon and aluminum presented in the fly ash to allow the glassy structure to be partially or totally dissolved and transformed into a very compacted composite (Guo et al., 2010). However, leftover fly ash particles started to be further dissolved with increasing the $\\mathrm{{NaOH}}$ concentration. At ${10}\\mathrm{\\;{mol}}/\\mathrm{l}$ , unreacted fly ash particles were substantially reduced and were almost completely dissolved at ${12}\\mathrm{\\;{mol}}/\\mathrm{l}$ and 14 mol/l. In addition, with such optimum conditions, pre-existing crack of geopolymer could be reduced significantly. According to Griffith's principle, specimens containing larger pre-existing crack would exhibit low compressive strength. The use of ${12}\\mathrm{\\;{mol}}/\\mathrm{l}$ $\\mathrm{{NaOH}}$ solution resulted in smooth surface, less existing crack, and less existence of unreacted fly ash particles. Therefore, this concentration used as alkaline activator for the preparation of fly ash based geopolymer-mullite composites.\n![01973e1a-dfde-72a9-84ae-b84e3d3bb8ca_4_380_834_869_529_0.jpg](images/01973e1a-dfde-72a9-84ae-b84e3d3bb8ca_4_380_834_869_529_0.jpg)", 'chunk_index': 4, 'chunk_metadata': {'strategy': 'semantic', 'type': 'ContentType.TEXT', 'headings': [], 'page_number': [1], 'extra': None}}
