2025-07-11 21:46:41 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:175 - 创建分块 6: 数据键 = ['id', 'document_id', 'content', 'chunk_index', 'chunk_metadata']
2025-07-11 21:46:41 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:176 - 创建分块 6: 数据 = {'id': '9b029803-e127-4da2-8c59-31f0524a5a2f', 'document_id': '8f0e4010-d70a-4f47-9129-b82d8760cbc2', 'content': 'the components. This slurry was then put in a planetary conditioning mixer that further mixed and degassed for removal of fine bubbles. The geopolymerization reaction can be summed up as follows: ## ${\\mathrm{K}}_{2}\\mathrm{O} \\cdot 2{\\mathrm{{SiO}}}_{2} \\cdot {11}{\\mathrm{H}}_{2}\\mathrm{O} + 2{\\mathrm{{SiO}}}_{2} \\cdot {\\mathrm{{Al}}}_{2}{\\mathrm{O}}_{3} \\rightarrow {\\mathrm{K}}_{2}\\mathrm{O} \\cdot {\\mathrm{{Al}}}_{2}{\\mathrm{O}}_{3} \\cdot 4{\\mathrm{{SiO}}}_{2} \\cdot {11}{\\mathrm{H}}_{2}\\mathrm{O}$ (2) Seven ( ${155} \\times {25} \\times 4\\mathrm{\\;{mm}}$ ) bamboo-reinforced geopolymer composite samples (Fig. 2(b)) were subjected to third-point loading flexural strength testing according to ASTM standard C1341- ${13}^{24}$ . The average density of bamboo-reinforced geopolymer composites was ${1.35}\\mathrm{\\;g}{\\mathrm{\\;{cm}}}^{-3}$ . Tests were carried out by an Instron testing machine at the Advanced Mechanical Testing and Evaluation Laboratory (AMTEL) of UIUC. The test span to depth ratio was 25:1, and the crosshead displacement rate was ${0.10}\\mathrm{\\;{mm}}{\\mathrm{\\;s}}^{-1}$ . Strain was measured by crosshead displacement.\n![01973e1a-dfe2-7052-97bf-a3fb626b5a97_3_206_1229_888_245_0.jpg](images/01973e1a-dfe2-7052-97bf-a3fb626b5a97_3_206_1229_888_245_0.jpg)\nFigure 2. Bamboo-reinforced geopolymer composite (a) plate and (b) tested specimens.\nWeibull functions were calculated using a linear regression method. A distribution function, $\\mathrm{F}$ , was estimated based on the flexure strength of $\\mathrm{n}$ samples using the median rank method shown in the following equation:\n$$ F \\cong \\frac{i - {0.3}}{n + {0.4}} \\tag{3} $$\nwhere the flexure strength for each sample is given a rank $i$ from 1 to $\\mathrm{n}$ , which is the number of samples in the batch. The Weibull distribution function is given by equation (4), where $\\mathrm{v}$ is the non-dimensional volume, $\\sigma$ is the stress, ${\\sigma }_{0}$ is the scale parameter and $m$ is the shape parameter or Weibull modulus:', 'chunk_index': 6, 'chunk_metadata': {'strategy': 'semantic', 'type': 'ContentType.TEXT', 'headings': [], 'page_number': [1], 'extra': None}}
