2025-07-11 22:04:05 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:176 - 创建分块 20: 数据 = {'id': 'f61b44e6-c738-4bd7-9e2d-4a9879b817f9', 'document_id': 'd36b7122-2414-41cc-bb70-d36713287425', 'content': 'Table 4) as cure activators, accelerating the sulfur vulcanization of NR, ${}^{\\left\\lbrack {10}\\right\\rbrack }$ so that also the cure rate index increased with geopolymer loading. In addition, it is clearly seen that the cure rate index of the compound with ${10}\\mathrm{{phr}}\\mathrm{{GP}}$ was much lower than with 10 phr FA. ![01974484-c8d5-72ac-a6d1-acf6a850ed04_6_127_126_1513_788_0.jpg](images/01974484-c8d5-72ac-a6d1-acf6a850ed04_6_127_126_1513_788_0.jpg) Figure 6 shows light microscopy images of gum together with GP- and FA-filled NR composites. It is seen that the gum NR vulcanizate had smoother fractured surfaces with smaller white $\\mathrm{{ZnO}}$ particles. In contrast, the fractured surfaces of the NR/GP and NR/FA composites were rougher because of some geopolymer or fly ash agglomerates. The dispersion with ${10}\\mathrm{{phr}}\\mathrm{{GP}}$ in the $\\mathrm{{NR}}$ composite was more homogeneous than that observed for the NR/FA (10 phr) composite. This might be attributed to the weaker filler-matrix interactions in NR/FA (Figure 6b) than in NR/GP composites (Figure 6c), as indicated by some voids or cavities in the light microscopy images of the former. In Figure 6(e, f), it is also seen that strong and large geopolymer agglomerates were clear in the NR-GP30 and NR-GP40 composites. This might be due to excessive filler loading and poor adhesion of GP particles with the NR matrix. ${}^{\\left\\lbrack {10}\\right\\rbrack }$ Higher magnification SEM micrographs are shown in Figure 7. Smooth surfaces with tiny white spots of $\\mathrm{{ZnO}}$ were confirmed for the gum $\\mathrm{{NR}}$ vulcanizate (Figure 7a). Furthermore, large GP agglomerates, cavities and voids around the GP aggregates are clear in composites with 30 or 40 phr GP loading (Figure 6e, f). Thus, it can be concluded that the optimum geopolymer loading in a NR composite might be in the range from 10 to ${20}\\mathrm{{phr}}$ .', 'chunk_index': 20, 'chunk_metadata': {'strategy': 'semantic', 'type': 'ContentType.TEXT', 'headings': [], 'page_number': [1], 'extra': None}}
