2025-07-11 22:04:05 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:176 - 创建分块 22: 数据 = {'id': '862429b4-160a-4382-b9fd-504ee4cd34b2', 'document_id': 'd36b7122-2414-41cc-bb70-d36713287425', 'content': '$\\left( {{M}_{\\mathrm{H}},\\mathrm{d}\\mathrm{{Nm}}}\\right)$</td><td>Delta torque $\\left( {{\\mathbf{M}}_{\\mathbf{H}}\\mathbf{ - }{\\mathbf{M}}_{\\mathbf{L}}\\mathbf{, d}\\mathbf{N}\\mathbf{m}}\\right)$</td><td>Scorch time (ts2, min)</td><td>Cure time (tc90, min)</td><td>Cure rate index $\\left( {\\mathbf{{CRI}},{\\mathbf{{min}}}^{-1}}\\right)$</td></tr><tr><td>Gum NR</td><td>1.55</td><td>8.64</td><td>7.09</td><td>2.17</td><td>7.12</td><td>20.2</td></tr><tr><td>NR-FA10</td><td>1.24</td><td>8.29</td><td>7.05</td><td>1.19</td><td>4.33</td><td>31.8</td></tr><tr><td>NR-GP10</td><td>2.06</td><td>9.57</td><td>7.51</td><td>0.54</td><td>4.49</td><td>25.3</td></tr><tr><td>NR-GP20</td><td>2.01</td><td>8.90</td><td>6.89</td><td>1.03</td><td>4.08</td><td>32.8</td></tr><tr><td>NR-GP30</td><td>2.05</td><td>8.52</td><td>6.45</td><td>0.58</td><td>2.59</td><td>49.8</td></tr><tr><td>NR-GP40</td><td>2.01</td><td>8.60</td><td>6.59</td><td>0.58</td><td>2.57</td><td>50.3</td></tr></table> ![01974484-c8d5-72ac-a6d1-acf6a850ed04_7_128_1449_1010_753_0.jpg](images/01974484-c8d5-72ac-a6d1-acf6a850ed04_7_128_1449_1010_753_0.jpg) FIGURE 8 Schematic representations of geopolymer (GP) and fly ash (FA) structures and their dispersion in the natural rubber (NR) matrix, for the NR/GP (a) and NR/FA (b) composites ![01974484-c8d5-72ac-a6d1-acf6a850ed04_8_626_133_1014_781_0.jpg](images/01974484-c8d5-72ac-a6d1-acf6a850ed04_8_626_133_1014_781_0.jpg) FIGURE 9 Stress-strain curves of gum, fly ash- and geopolymer (GP)-filled natural rubber vulcanizates with various geopolymer loadings or 10 phr fly ash. (The insert provides magnified view of below 100% extensions.)\n### 3.5 | Mechanical properties\nFigure 9 shows the stress-strain curves for the gum, and the FA- and GP-filled NR vulcanizates with various geo-polymer loadings or with ${10}\\mathrm{{phr}}$ fly ash. It is seen that the NR composites with 10 or ${20}\\mathrm{{phr}}\\mathrm{{GP}}$ exhibited higher Young’s and ${100}\\%$ moduli (i.e., final positions in the graph insert) and stress-strain curves raised above that of the gum vulcanizate. However, incorporating more than 20 phr GP (or 10 phr FA) gave similar stress-strain curves to the gum vulcanizate but with different failure location or elongation at break. The geopolymer at 10 or ${20}\\mathrm{{phr}}$ reduced the mobility of molecular chains and hence increased stiffness of the material. In contrast, incorporating 30 or 40 phr GP (or 10 phr FA) lowered the ${100}\\%$ modulus and the stress-strain curve from those of the gum NR vulcanizate. This might be due to agglomeration of filler (Figures 6 and 7) which reduced effective specific surface and reinforcing performance. This result agrees well with the high delta torque for the NR composite with ${10}\\mathrm{{phr}}\\mathrm{{GP}}$ (Table 6).', 'chunk_index': 22, 'chunk_metadata': {'strategy': 'semantic', 'type': 'ContentType.TEXT', 'headings': [], 'page_number': [1], 'extra': None}}
