2025-07-11 22:04:05 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:175 - 创建分块 23: 数据键 = ['id', 'document_id', 'content', 'chunk_index', 'chunk_metadata']
2025-07-11 22:04:05 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:176 - 创建分块 23: 数据 = {'id': '16c6cb82-f5ae-4264-84b3-f49cdabb9764', 'document_id': 'd36b7122-2414-41cc-bb70-d36713287425', 'content': '${100}\\%$ modulus and the stress-strain curve from those of the gum NR vulcanizate. This might be due to agglomeration of filler (Figures 6 and 7) which reduced effective specific surface and reinforcing performance. This result agrees well with the high delta torque for the NR composite with ${10}\\mathrm{{phr}}\\mathrm{{GP}}$ (Table 6). The mechanical properties tensile strength and elongation at break, obtained from the failure points of the stress-strain curves in Figure 9, are plotted in Figure 10. It can be seen that the GP/NR (10 phr) vulcanizate exhibited higher tensile strength but lower elongation at break than the gum NR vulcanizate. This may be due to the interactions of geopolymer and NR that improved the strength but reduced the ability to extend. Furthermore, further increase in geopolymer loading decreased the tensile strength and the elongation at break. This again might be due to excessive filler loading creating flaws that initiate failure: voids, cavities, and filler aggregates (Figures 6 and 7). The lack of adhesion between rubber matrix and filler allowed formation of weak spots that degraded tensile strength and elongation at break. ${}^{\\left\\lbrack {31}\\right\\rbrack }$\n![01974484-c8d5-72ac-a6d1-acf6a850ed04_8_624_1485_1014_714_0.jpg](images/01974484-c8d5-72ac-a6d1-acf6a850ed04_8_624_1485_1014_714_0.jpg)\nFIGURE 10 Tensile strength and elongation at break for the gum, and for fly ash- or geopolymer-filled natural rubber vulcanizates with various geopolymer loadings or with 10 phr fly ash\nFIGURE 11 Storage modulus as a function of strain amplitude for the gum, and for fly ash- or geopolymer-filled natural rubber vulcanizates with various geopolymer loadings or 10 phr fly ash, tested at ${80}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ and $1\\mathrm{\\;{Hz}}$', 'chunk_index': 23, 'chunk_metadata': {'strategy': 'semantic', 'type': 'ContentType.TEXT', 'headings': [], 'page_number': [1], 'extra': None}}
2025-07-11 22:04:05 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:189 - 分块 23 创建成功: 16c6cb82-f5ae-4264-84b3-f49cdabb9764
2025-07-11 22:04:05 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:175 - 创建分块 24: 数据键 = ['id', 'document_id', 'content', 'chunk_index', 'chunk_metadata']
2025-07-11 22:04:05 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:176 - 创建分块 24: 数据 = {'id': 'e7ee1d34-2abe-4d5c-8728-c477196c8bf0', 'document_id': 'd36b7122-2414-41cc-bb70-d36713287425', 'content': 'rubber vulcanizates with various geopolymer loadings or with 10 phr fly ash FIGURE 11 Storage modulus as a function of strain amplitude for the gum, and for fly ash- or geopolymer-filled natural rubber vulcanizates with various geopolymer loadings or 10 phr fly ash, tested at ${80}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ and $1\\mathrm{\\;{Hz}}$ The storage modulus of a filled polymer composite is typically influenced by the interfacial interactions of inorganic filler particles with polymer matrix. Strain-induced filler-filler network breakdown typically occurs during dynamic mechanical experiments at a constant frequency and temperature. Figure 11 shows the storage modulus as a function of strain amplitude for the gum, and the FA- and GP-filled NR vulcanizates with various geopolymer loadings or with ${10}\\mathrm{{phr}}$ fly ash, tested at ${80}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ and $1\\mathrm{\\;{Hz}}$ . Nearly linear viscoelastic behavior was observed for the gum NR vulcanizate, whereas nonlinear viscoelastic behavior was seen with both GP/NR and FA/NR composites. This might be attributed to the breakdown of filler-filler networks known as the Payne effect. ${}^{\\left\\lbrack {32}\\right\\rbrack }$ It is noted that ${\\mathrm{G}}^{\\prime }$ is a measure of the energy stored/released mechanically during cyclic deformations, and it decreased monotonically with the strain amplitude in all cases. Generally, the absolute value of ${\\mathrm{G}}^{\\prime }$ in the low-strain regime relates to filler-filler networks. It is seen that ${\\mathrm{G}}^{\\prime }$ in the low-strain regime for the GP-filled NR composites increased with filler loading, while the FA-filled NR composite had the lowest value. This implies that the filler-filler networks grew with geopolymer loading while the FA filler networks were weaker. It is noted that the network formation by a filler in NR matrix is strongly related to the specific surface area and the self-aggregation tendency.', 'chunk_index': 24, 'chunk_metadata': {'strategy': 'semantic', 'type': 'ContentType.TEXT', 'headings': [], 'page_number': [1], 'extra': None}}
