2025-07-11 22:04:06 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:175 - 创建分块 0: 数据键 = ['id', 'document_id', 'content', 'chunk_index', 'chunk_metadata']
2025-07-11 22:04:06 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:176 - 创建分块 0: 数据 = {'id': 'fda4af71-16e7-452a-bd20-328c37b03975', 'document_id': '58b1d2d2-05dd-4b74-98c0-d84578c57cec', 'content': '## Mechanical and physical properties of fly ash geopolymer-mullite composites\n![01973e1a-dfde-72a9-84ae-b84e3d3bb8ca_0_104_437_1375_676_0.jpg](images/01973e1a-dfde-72a9-84ae-b84e3d3bb8ca_0_104_437_1375_676_0.jpg)\n# MECHANICAL AND PHYSICAL PROPERTIES OF FLY ASH GEOPOLYMER - MULLITE COMPOSITES\nFaisal Arif Nurgesang ${}^{1}$ , Suthee Wattanasiriwech ${}^{1}$ *, Darunee Wattanasiriwech ${}^{1}$ and Pavadee Aungkavattana ${}^{2}$\nReceived: July 24, 2015; Revised: September 26, 2105; Accepted: February 16, 2016\n## Abstract\nThis work aims to investigate how compressive strength of a typical class $\\mathrm{C}$ fly ash-based geopolymer would be changed with addition of mullite. In preparation of basic geopolymer, fly ash was collected from Mae Moh power plants, Lampang province, was alkaline activated and cured for a total period of 7 days. In making the composites, 20-60% of mullite powder was added to the basic geopolymer mixture and cured at the same condition. Porosity, density, and water absorption of the samples were determined by following ASTM C-642. The results showed that the compressive strength of the geopolymer mortar could be remarkably improved from 14 MPa to 71 MPa with mullite addition. The mechanical property variation of the composites was discussed along with their physical properties and microstructures.\nKeywords: Geopolymer, class $\\mathrm{C}$ fly ash-based geopolymer, fly ash geopolymer-mullite composites\n## Introduction', 'chunk_index': 0, 'chunk_metadata': {'strategy': 'semantic', 'type': 'ContentType.TEXT', 'headings': [], 'page_number': [1], 'extra': {'paragraph_index': 0, 'original_length': 78}}}
2025-07-11 22:04:06 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:189 - 分块 0 创建成功: fda4af71-16e7-452a-bd20-328c37b03975
2025-07-11 22:04:06 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:175 - 创建分块 1: 数据键 = ['id', 'document_id', 'content', 'chunk_index', 'chunk_metadata']
2025-07-11 22:04:06 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:176 - 创建分块 1: 数据 = {'id': '93929f70-5a3e-4b92-b93a-a74dc7d8ceb4', 'document_id': '58b1d2d2-05dd-4b74-98c0-d84578c57cec', 'content': 'that the compressive strength of the geopolymer mortar could be remarkably improved from 14 MPa to 71 MPa with mullite addition. The mechanical property variation of the composites was discussed along with their physical properties and microstructures. Keywords: Geopolymer, class $\\mathrm{C}$ fly ash-based geopolymer, fly ash geopolymer-mullite composites ## Introduction In May 2015, the NOAA/Earth System Research Laboratory has announced that the level of average global atmospheric ${\\mathrm{{CO}}}_{2}$ concentration reached a new high of ${401}\\mathrm{{ppm}}$ (Dlugokencky and Tans, 2015). This alarmed us that we need to deal with ${\\mathrm{{CO}}}_{2}$ emission more seriously. There are several sources that can contribute to ${\\mathrm{{CO}}}_{2}$ emission in the world. Cement industry from all over the world generates about 7% of global ${\\mathrm{{CO}}}_{2}$ emission (Davidovits,1994). Nowadays, geopolymer is a promising product that can either partially or totally replace cement (Akbari et al., 2015 and Wanjari et al., 2015). Using geopolymer helps not only reduce ${\\mathrm{{CO}}}_{2}$ emission by reducing cement production, but also utilize waste material such as fly ash (Palomo et al., 1999). Fly ash is a byproduct from coal combustion process that abundant over a total of 600 metric ton per year (Joshi et al., 1997). Utilization of class $\\mathrm{C}$ fly ash for making geopolymer was widely studied but commonly achieved low compressive strength (Guo et al., 2010; Zheng et al., 2010; Somna et al., 2011; Li et al., 2013; Abdulkareem et al., 2014; Bagheri and Nazari, 2014; Abdollahnejad et al., 2015; Embong et al., 2015). Therefore, engineering class $\\mathrm{C}$ fly ash based geopolymer for high compressive strength applications is a great challenge. In order to realize that, it might need to be reinforced or composited.\n---', 'chunk_index': 1, 'chunk_metadata': {'strategy': 'semantic', 'type': 'ContentType.TEXT', 'headings': [], 'page_number': [1], 'extra': None}}
