2025-07-11 22:04:06 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:176 - 创建分块 3: 数据 = {'id': 'acf9703c-827c-4fc6-a284-75b2c6c5604d', 'document_id': '58b1d2d2-05dd-4b74-98c0-d84578c57cec', 'content': 'diffraction analysis (Malvern Instrument Mastersizer, 2000) as presented in TABLE 2. In this study, a mixture of chemical-grade sodium hydroxide solution and commercial sodium silicate was used as the alkaline activator. ![01973e1a-dfde-72a9-84ae-b84e3d3bb8ca_2_212_1095_1193_410_0.jpg](images/01973e1a-dfde-72a9-84ae-b84e3d3bb8ca_2_212_1095_1193_410_0.jpg) Figure 1. SEM pictures of raw materials ![01973e1a-dfde-72a9-84ae-b84e3d3bb8ca_2_437_1593_766_507_0.jpg](images/01973e1a-dfde-72a9-84ae-b84e3d3bb8ca_2_437_1593_766_507_0.jpg) Figure 2. X-ray diffractogram of raw materials ## Sample Preparation The optimum concentration of sodium hydroxide solution in preparation of alkaline activator was predetermined by mixing various sodium hydroxide solutions ranging from 8-16 $\\mathrm{{mol}}/1$ with sodium silicate. Dry fly ash was mixed with alkaline activator and hand stirred for 1 minute. The mixture was then poured into cylindrical PVC molds ( $\\varnothing {15}\\mathrm{\\;{mm}} \\times {30}\\mathrm{\\;{mm}}$ ). The geopolymer-mullite composite was prepared by adding mullite powder, ranging from 20-60 wt% of dry fly ash, to the optimized geopolymer mixture followed by the same curing procedure. The detail of various mixtures of fly ash-based geopolymer and its composites with mullite is given in TABLE 3. The samples were cured at ${90}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ for 1 day and then at ${40}^{ \\circ }\\mathrm{C}$ for 6 days.\n## Setting Time and Compressive Strength\nThe setting time of geopolymer paste was tested according to ASTM C-191 with a manually operated standard Vicat apparatus. The compressive strength was measured after 7 days of curing. The samples were demolded from cylindrical PVC molds, and subjected to the compressive test by using Universal Testing Machine (Instron 5566) with the loading speed of $1\\mathrm{\\;{mm}}/\\mathrm{{min}}$ . Each reported value is the average of five samples.\n## Scanning Electron Microscopy', 'chunk_index': 3, 'chunk_metadata': {'strategy': 'semantic', 'type': 'ContentType.TEXT', 'headings': [], 'page_number': [1], 'extra': None}}
