2025-07-11 22:04:06 | INFO     | db.repositories.knowledge_repository:create_chunks:176 - 创建分块 9: 数据 = {'id': '2402bb7e-475d-4c7e-9080-b1dcb4414fdc', 'document_id': '58b1d2d2-05dd-4b74-98c0-d84578c57cec', 'content': 'ratio</td></tr><tr><td>Fly ash</td><td colspan="2">Addition of mullite</td><td colspan="2">NaOH</td><td>${\\mathrm{{Na}}}_{2}{\\mathrm{{SiO}}}_{3}$</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>${40}\\mathrm{\\;g}$</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td>8-16 mol/1</td><td>${12}\\mathrm{\\;g}$</td><td>${12}\\mathrm{\\;g}$</td><td>${64}\\mathrm{\\;g}$</td><td>$1 : {0.60}$</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>${40}\\mathrm{\\;g}$</td><td>20 wt%</td><td>$8\\mathrm{\\;g}$</td><td>Selected</td><td>${12}\\mathrm{\\;g}$</td><td>${12}\\mathrm{\\;g}$</td><td>72 g</td><td>$1 : {0.50}$</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>${40}\\mathrm{\\;g}$</td><td>40 wt%</td><td>16 g</td><td>Selected</td><td>${12}\\mathrm{\\;g}$</td><td>${12}\\mathrm{\\;g}$</td><td>80 g</td><td>1 : 0.42</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>40 g</td><td>60 wt%</td><td>${24}\\mathrm{\\;g}$</td><td>Selected</td><td>12 g</td><td>${12}\\mathrm{\\;g}$</td><td>88 g</td><td>$1 : {0.37}$</td></tr></table> Table 4. Physical properties of geopolymers <table><tr><td>Geopolymers</td><td>Bulk density (g/cm3)</td><td>Apparent density (g/cm3)</td><td>Volume of permeable voids (%)</td><td>Water absorption (%)</td></tr><tr><td>FA-Geopolymer</td><td>1.65</td><td>2.21</td><td>24.9</td><td>12.86</td></tr><tr><td>FA-M 40 wt%</td><td>1.81</td><td>2.24</td><td>19.0</td><td>9.90</td></tr></table> ## Density, Voids, and Apparent Density Measurement Table 4 presents physical property of fly ash and fly ash-mullite composites geopolymer. Fly ash-based geopolymer has lower bulk and apparent density, and relatively high volume of permeable voids at 24.9%. This existing voids tend to absorbs more water. In contrast, fly ash geopolymer -mullite composite has higher bulk and apparent density, low volume of permeable voids, and low water absorption. By adding ${40}\\mathrm{{wt}}\\%$ of mullite, the permeable voids was reduce to 5% with only 3% of water absorption. It indicates that mullite addition could lead to more dense and low porosity material than fly ash-based geopolymer.\n## Conclusions\nThe concentration of alkaline activator affected compressive strength of geopolymers. At low concentration of $8 - {10}\\mathrm{\\;{mol}}/\\mathrm{l}$ , the $\\mathrm{{NaOH}}$ solution could not effectively dissolve fly ash particles. However, when the concentration was increased to ${12}\\mathrm{{mol}}/\\mathrm{l}$ , the fly ash was successfully dissolved, resulting in an average compressive strength with great consistency of 14 MPa. The addition of mullite into fly ash-based geopolymer could increase the compressive strength significantly i.e., specimen with 40 wt% of mullite addition reached an average of ${71}\\mathrm{{MPa}}$ . This could be due to the presence of mullite crystal effectively reinforcing the matrix of the composite system. The addition of mullite could also reduce permeable voids and water absorption of the geopolymer.', 'chunk_index': 9, 'chunk_metadata': {'strategy': 'semantic', 'type': 'ContentType.TEXT', 'headings': [], 'page_number': [1], 'extra': None}}
